    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "Why does a standard DCF use unlevered free cash flow rather than levered free cash flow?",
    a: "Because it values the operating business independently of how it's financed. Unlevered FCF is the cash available to every capital provider, before interest and debt repayments, so discounting it at WACC gives enterprise value: comparable across peers and relevant to any buyer, whatever capital structure they plan to use.",
    why: "Financing changes who gets the cash, not how much the operations produce. A buyer will often refinance the target, so today's debt schedule says little about what the business is worth. Levered cash flow also jumps around with each borrowing and repayment, which makes it harder to forecast and to compare. The unlevered approach splits the problem in two: the cash flows capture operations, and WACC captures financing, including the tax benefit of debt through the after-tax cost of debt. The bridge then divides enterprise value between lenders and shareholders.",
    trap: "The follow-up: when would you use levered cash flow? For banks and insurers, where borrowing is part of the business, and when you want equity value directly under a financing plan that's already fixed."
