    q: "What should you check in the final forecast year before calculating the terminal value?",
    a: "That it's a normal year the business could repeat forever: growth at or near the terminal rate; margins at a sustainable level; capex a bit above D&A; working capital growing with revenue; taxes at a full rate, with no NOLs, one-offs or temporary benefits. If it isn't clean, add a normalized terminal-year column rather than capitalizing it as is.",
    why: "The terminal value capitalizes one year's cash flow into perpetuity, so any distortion in that year is multiplied by the perpetuity multiple, about 17x at a 9% WACC and 3% growth, instead of counting once. Reinvestment must match growth, because a company can't grow forever while spending only what it depreciates. Working capital should absorb only what the terminal growth rate requires. For a cyclical, the terminal margin should be mid-cycle, even if the forecast ends at a peak.",
    example: "Final-year FCF of [[$120M]] includes a [[$15M]] one-time benefit. At a [[9%]] WACC and [[3%]] growth, capitalizing it overstates the terminal value by $15M × 1.03 ÷ 6% = $257.5M.",
