    why: "D&A reflects what assets cost years ago, so replacing them at today's prices costs more, and growth needs new capacity on top. Forecasts often end with capex below D&A because a big investment program is tapering off, which flatters the final year's cash flow. Capitalized into perpetuity, the gap is large. A common convention sets terminal capex at a modest premium to D&A, or sizes total reinvestment to fit the growth rate and a realistic return on new capital.",
