    a: "About 3.3%. Solve the Gordon formula for growth: (terminal value × WACC − final-year FCF) ÷ (terminal value + final-year FCF). It tells you what the multiple assumes about the business forever. At 3.3%, the multiple is at the rich end of what long-run growth can support; well above 4% would mean the multiple is too high.",
    why: "A multiple is a compressed DCF: it embeds growth, risk and reinvestment. Turning it back into a perpetual growth rate lets you judge it on fundamentals. If peers trade at 12x because the market expects years of fast growth, applying 12x to a mature terminal year imports that growth forever. The check runs both ways: a Gordon growth value should imply a multiple in line with mature peers. When the two methods disagree, look for a multiple that reflects today's market or a growth rate that's too conservative.",
