    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What is adjusted present value (APV), and when would you use it instead of a WACC-based DCF?",
    a: "APV values the business as if it had no debt, discounting unlevered free cash flow at the unlevered cost of equity, then adds the present value of the interest tax shields, less any expected distress costs. Use it when the capital structure will change sharply, as in an LBO, a recapitalization or project finance, where a single WACC doesn't fit.",
    why: "WACC folds the tax benefit of debt into the discount rate, which works only if debt stays a roughly constant share of value. When debt starts high and is paid down, the right WACC changes every year, and a single rate misstates value. APV separates the two sources of value: the operations, discounted at the rate for an all-equity company, and the financing effects, valued on their own schedule. It also shows how much of the value comes from financing, which helps you judge a deal.",
    formula: "APV = unlevered value + PV of interest tax shields − PV of expected distress costs\nAnnual tax shield = interest expense × tax rate",
    example: "Unlevered value [[$1,000M]]. Permanent debt of [[$400M]] at [[6%]] with a [[25%]] tax rate saves $6M of tax a year. Discounted at the 6% cost of debt, that's worth $100M, or tax rate × debt. APV: $1,100M before distress costs.",
    trap: "Don't discount the operations at WACC and then add the tax shields too: WACC already includes them through the after-tax cost of debt. APV's operating discount rate is the unlevered cost of equity."
